#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124304 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124491 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "595fd527-e8fd-5360-87f1-acbeab511566" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Given a Linear Connection Between Two Bases"}] :question/id #uuid "4c1e01c1-d607-5ad8-8643-b3317201ab9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4832" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2\\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\{\\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2\\}$ be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ If $\\mathbf{c}_1=\\mathbf{b}_2-4\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2=5\\mathbf{b}_1+7\\mathbf{b}_2,$ find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $\\mathcal{C}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are\n![](images/da/daf4111656a525abf51bf58105831ed11378ed7fc52915e1e58ca5f75884bcf0.svg)\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{C}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](images/be/bed6471ce9aed8eb16a0d35f1d9985b8e99941f7c1326645de971960bdb5aedd.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124305 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b973d79-8fe4-5033-8202-9693004855dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix When One of the Bases is the Standard Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "93b466b2-9b8d-55fa-87b8-b5bf24a497cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-4833" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and let $\\mathcal{S}$ be the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ Find the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n![](images/95/957c59d27a871a52f110c3dca91cec390091c13ceba6e14d6485f92aa150c51a.svg)\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is \n\n![](images/60/6095f1a65a9b9b245de2dd4cad611d2616a2b3b15e4701fe33c3c53b913c2283.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consists of the vectors $\\mathbf e_1, \\mathbf e_2,$ and $\\mathbf e_3,$ given by\n![](images/41/41bd0d4956d8ec09d0339a79329c6739e07e698d9dee9f192968d77c9ec28842.svg)\n\nThe components of the vectors in $\\mathcal{B}$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n![](images/30/30a1bdc2316f5d7acc527cb1d69fc11c7b732f9e22a4d57a11ed84f2db1e3cd5.svg)\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{S}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](images/56/5606e00ccc39829e3c599aa86286dcc1f1cb4e3febb280adf4e1bbe18dc7ab6a.svg)\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=0,$ and $c=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124306 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124493 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23b6a85e-e1de-590c-84d2-379052b1b9f3" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Invertibility Property of the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "6c5b5534-2082-593e-af07-df83497ea84c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4835" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by \n![](images/46/4676de86bd5b4bc2a8e1d6a9432379d0769163113913137f8143a71eb16e8240.svg)\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot 5-(-6)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2} & -3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124307 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f989cbaf-2d99-5267-8864-48a48db2d535" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Between Two Bases"}] :question/id #uuid "0929855b-0310-5c87-8cff-cc2faa8a5ae0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4837" :question/problem "Let ![](images/07/076634439afec4c0e4fb0a443d88163245269988e323ddb9d83d1a4a1b18139e.svg) and ![](images/b2/b2fd30034f45fb2d8b06f2697f137c93de21942822b08193c3e640ef51d4e7c0.svg) be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\},$ respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nSo, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](images/dd/dd733ebebc7131eb2762e6e98e1d14d07a730e5e37657c639ae9cc46be123ee2.svg)\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & 22 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}= & [\\begin{aligned}\\,4 & 22 \\\\ \\,1 & 13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124493 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23b6a85e-e1de-590c-84d2-379052b1b9f3" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Invertibility Property of the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c504131c-23c0-5777-871f-f40a82fa2c85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122085} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f092b2-afac-5f15-856b-c39dc2668472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122086} {:answer/id #uuid "fdabfb8f-8209-5c6f-84ec-a59adc5acf8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -\\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122087} {:answer/id #uuid "0c1d841f-61a5-535a-a4f4-6f463f0347bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -\\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122088} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb25a07-b1eb-5ee6-988b-c307f04d8580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122089}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bd4a1d79-e29c-55e2-9104-304e27e73d47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122084}] :question/id #uuid "3bc8c503-4f0a-519e-810b-af533bd26af8" :question/math-academy-id "q-35793" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ is given by\n$P_{B → C} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416596 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124491 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "595fd527-e8fd-5360-87f1-acbeab511566" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Given a Linear Connection Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2344242d-6b96-5993-b8b0-de107b4279a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=4,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416533} {:answer/id #uuid "3879c262-51c9-5503-937f-270c4a66ac2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=4,$ $b=1,$ and $c=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416537} {:answer/id #uuid "54569e6c-7221-5713-add5-c98abf257a21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=4,$ and $c=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416545} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c37f7a-6b0a-5951-912a-c0791c4bd8ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=0,$ and $c=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416567} {:answer/id #uuid "b19c2423-673d-5c2f-9566-5866776b9ddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=4,$ and $c=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2344242d-6b96-5993-b8b0-de107b4279a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=4,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416533} :answer-field/id #uuid "4928869d-0a7f-5e17-a81c-6aaf1def0a0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30d93e6c-378b-5de5-9291-694547fd0977" :question/math-academy-id "q-36354" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ be bases of ${R}^{3}.$ \nWhat are the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & =2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}, \\\\ {b}_{2} & =4{c}_{1}-2{c}_{3}, \\\\ {b}_{3} & =2{c}_{1}-3{c}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a & 2 \\\\ b & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & c & -3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $B$ relative to the basis $C$ are\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{3}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=4,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323799 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124491 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "595fd527-e8fd-5360-87f1-acbeab511566" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Given a Linear Connection Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13508c3a-b778-5da9-944c-a0e84a3dd9b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321297} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf26bc4-131a-5b43-b454-72c6b6e9630d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322272} {:answer/id #uuid "923588e2-7ae8-5837-ae1d-a537b62e5d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322308} {:answer/id #uuid "f37ec7a1-c181-53d6-8795-c6c53eebd62e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323059} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e0f297-0165-5f20-bbd5-d9e2c5c3d348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f37ec7a1-c181-53d6-8795-c6c53eebd62e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323059} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7f66aa0-94fa-514c-baa2-1e24130ae6ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b80daa7e-24b9-50c1-a8b6-05bfcb214049" :question/math-academy-id "q-36355" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ If ${b}_{1}=5{c}_{2}-3{c}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}=2{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2},$ find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ from $B$ to $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $B$ relative to the basis $C$ are\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\,[{b}_{2}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b973d79-8fe4-5033-8202-9693004855dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix When One of the Bases is the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0f2de09-e3ad-512b-8e74-3a12edf3480d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394293} {:answer/id #uuid "1fcb9a14-b5a6-5c03-9ec6-27fac7199ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 11 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394294} {:answer/id #uuid "9f078de7-6bf2-52c5-bbfd-2095ee4f5399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394295} {:answer/id #uuid "76983eb3-ee3b-59df-9616-86db07326c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394296} {:answer/id #uuid "9158d772-d565-5842-972a-4024fc2947d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 11 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f078de7-6bf2-52c5-bbfd-2095ee4f5399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394295} :answer-field/id #uuid "8055db6f-b8ff-53ba-9dfb-f141126526ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62dadad6-bc72-59c3-b335-faab5922669e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81847" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ \nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to S}$ from $B$ to $S$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors of $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 11\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124493 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23b6a85e-e1de-590c-84d2-379052b1b9f3" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Invertibility Property of the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb6b89d9-5ecc-523c-9fe0-a98270e715c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -3 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394307} {:answer/id #uuid "b48cca5a-57d5-5e3e-85fc-5f7371b9a168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394308} {:answer/id #uuid "ebadf3ae-54e1-5e2d-ac3c-70c2f31c63ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394309} {:answer/id #uuid "0f308fd4-f8a6-5e5f-860e-48792c8fbbdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394310} {:answer/id #uuid "00d59288-f8b5-5054-8c1f-b5518246d305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 3 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00d59288-f8b5-5054-8c1f-b5518246d305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 3 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394311} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d1bd593-f01f-5378-b1fe-6d41f9f01e52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a27a3c1e-ad3c-56e4-aa1a-d2aedbc7058d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81859" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $C$ to the basis $B$ is given by\n${P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{aligned}].$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$  ." :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C} & ={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot (-3)-2\\cdot (-9)}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 3 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f989cbaf-2d99-5267-8864-48a48db2d535" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "070ecd32-2b28-56a5-85bd-6a34995dde85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377853} {:answer/id #uuid "d38da1ce-9f22-5dc7-9d87-db349fd71d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377854} {:answer/id #uuid "0b419e01-bc76-5a21-9ceb-6f1d6f2a07cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377855} {:answer/id #uuid "9298715d-b632-5fb7-a00c-0b61f088dbc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377856} {:answer/id #uuid "82445d41-0f67-53ce-9020-32e4dd244a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}16 & -15 \\\\ -15 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9298715d-b632-5fb7-a00c-0b61f088dbc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377856} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab9e6ef9-e9ea-5c1f-ad73-6228ba8a6770" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "89bdb866-da97-5894-836a-c2ba9ba48e11" :question/math-academy-id "q-81910" :question/problem "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}]}
